const PAGE_HTML = "<h1>ShootFlow Design Decisions</h1>\n<p>ShootFlow is a personal tool and product concept for photographers and videographers who want more finished social content with less file-management and editing overhead. The first implementation layer is a conservative core engine that can be shared by a future Mac app, iPhone app, background agent, and cloud/GPU workers.</p>\n<h2>Product shape</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Start as a desktop-first Mac workflow because large RAW/video shoots, SD cards, and external SSDs are easiest to manage locally.</li>\n<li>Add iPhone capture/review later as a companion: approve selects, review grades, post drafts, and kick off exports.</li>\n<li>Keep local storage and originals as the source of truth. AI may suggest, rank, grade, crop, and assemble, but the system should make provenance and reversibility obvious.</li>\n<li>Treat Instagram Reels, carousels, and feed photos as first-class deliverables, not as a final export checkbox.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Safety invariants</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Never delete source media during ingest.</li>\n<li>Never overwrite an existing destination file with different bytes.</li>\n<li>Verify copies with SHA-256 before treating an import as complete.</li>\n<li>Write ingest manifests so later editing jobs can reference immutable originals and checksums.</li>\n<li>Reject symlink-based source or destination paths in the core importer. This is intentionally strict because camera-card imports are high-trust, high-value operations.</li>\n<li>Rank detected drives conservatively. Camera-card-like volumes are review-only sources; large writable SSDs\ncan be automatic destination candidates. Discovery is advisory and scans only reviewed mount roots, skipping\nsymlinks and files rather than following them. A source/destination route can be recommended only as\n<code>ready_for_review</code>; the Mac app still has to show the user the source, destination, no-delete note, and\nchecksum-manifest requirement before invoking ingest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Core architecture</h2>\n<p>The repo currently contains a Python core package because it is quick to test and can model the critical workflow contracts before native UI work starts.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>shootflow.ingest</code>: media discovery, import planning, checksum copy execution, and manifest writing.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.catalog</code>: manifest loading, path validation, media typing, and the boundary between safe\nimports and production planning.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.analysis</code>: VLM JSON loading that trusts the production plan instead of arbitrary model\noutput paths, validates analysis values, and binds story inputs to graded artifacts.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.storage</code>: drive/storage candidate ranking and reviewed import-route recommendation for future\nMac/iPhone device detection layers. It can pick a likely camera-card/source plus writable SSD/local\ndestination for UI review while preserving the rule that source media is never deleted and actual copies still\ngo through the checksum manifest importer.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.storage_discovery</code>: native-app-facing mounted-volume discovery. It scans caller-approved volume\nroots such as macOS <code>/Volumes</code>, skips symlinked or non-directory entries, reads filesystem capacity, and\nproduces conservative <code>StorageCandidate</code> records before handing off to the existing reviewed route\nrecommendation and capacity-gate logic.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.macos_volume_metadata</code>: native metadata handoff for a future Swift/Disk Arbitration shell. It\naccepts typed volume facts, validates capacity and boolean flags, skips system/network/missing/file-backed\nor symlinked mounts, and then feeds the same <code>StorageCandidate</code> route contract used by portable discovery.\nEjectable external drives count as removable for ranking, but the metadata adapter remains advisory: it\ndoes not copy, delete, eject, recursively scan, or bypass operator review.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.deliverables</code>: parallelizable job planning for graded intermediates and Instagram outputs.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production</code>: shoot-level execution planning with proxy, VLM analysis, scene grouping, look\ndevelopment, grading, and Instagram packaging waves.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_report</code>: a read-only post-ingest report layer. It reloads a verified\n<code>ingest_manifest.json</code>, builds the deterministic production plan, and summarizes asset counts,\nparallel/serial wave shape, and planned Instagram deliverables without writing analysis, graded, render,\nstatus, or deliverable files. This gives the future Mac UI a safe &quot;what happens next&quot; screen between\ncard offload and AI/color execution.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_status</code>: durable pre-render production progress. It\ninitializes and validates <code>production_status.json</code> against the deterministic\nproduction-plan fingerprint, preserving pending/running/succeeded/failed/\nretryable job state for proxy, analysis, grading, storyline, and packaging\nworkers without granting cleanup or media-write authority. Single-job\ncompare/write updates take the same local lock, check expected state/attempt\nand optional lease timestamp, replace exactly one status, and reject stale\nworkers before they can clobber newer progress.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_worker</code>: the safe pre-render worker contract. It consumes\nonly a trusted <code>ProductionPlan</code> plus matching <code>ProductionStatusManifest</code>, plans\ndependency-ready pending/retryable jobs, keeps blocked jobs visible with missing\ndependencies, claims one action as running, verifies declared output files\nbefore success, and reports retry/failure statuses without deleting source,\ngraded, render, or deliverable files. It also plans and persists stale-running\nlease expirations as retryable statuses through the same compare/write status\nhelper, using the running status timestamp as the lease token so active workers\nthat refresh their heartbeat are not clobbered.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_adapter</code>: the first pre-render adapter proof. Its\ndeterministic dry-run adapter consumes a trusted <code>ProductionWorkerAction</code> and\nwrites only the action&#39;s declared file artifacts as non-overwriting placeholder\nfiles. <code>metadata-production-local</code> uses the same action/status boundary to read\nonly workspace-bound trusted inputs and write deterministic metadata JSON to\ndeclared outputs, giving future proxy/VLM/color workers a safer local probe\nshape. <code>proxy-production-local</code> is the first media-byte local adapter: it\ncopies a trusted workspace input into the declared proxy output as a\nbyte-identical stand-in until real downsampled proxy generation lands.\n<code>analysis-production-local</code> is a deterministic local analysis probe. It writes\nloader-compatible <code>analysis/00000.json</code> outputs only for <code>vlm_asset_analysis</code>\njobs, deriving conservative scores from trusted input bytes so the status,\nchecksum, and downstream analysis-reader contracts can be exercised before\nreal VLM execution is trusted. <code>scene-production-local</code> is the next\ndeterministic serial-stage probe. It reads the trusted plan&#39;s validated\nper-asset analysis artifacts through <code>load_asset_analyses(...)</code>, verifies those\ndependency files against the succeeded analysis job checksums in\n<code>production_status.json</code>, and writes one declared <code>analysis/scene_groups.json</code>\nsummary for <code>scene_grouping</code> jobs only, proving the analysis-to-scene rejoin\nbefore real clustering workers are trusted. <code>look-production-local</code> is the next\ndeterministic serial-stage probe: it verifies the succeeded scene-grouping\noutput against <code>production_status.json</code> checksums, validates the scene summary\npayload shape and aggregate counts, and writes one declared\n<code>color/shoot_look.json</code> profile for <code>look_development</code> jobs only, proving the\nscene-to-look handoff before real color science or reference-look learning is\ntrusted. <code>grade-production-local</code> is the first per-asset color-stage probe: it\nverifies the trusted original, schema-validated/checksum-verified analysis\ndependency, and schema-validated/checksum-verified look dependency, then writes one\ndeterministic stand-in graded artifact for <code>graded_photo</code>/<code>graded_video</code> jobs\nonly. It proves the\nlook-to-grade handoff before ffmpeg/Core Image/Metal grading workers are\ntrusted. <code>story-production-local</code> is the deterministic editorial-stage probe:\nit verifies every planned analysis, scene, look, and graded input against\ntrusted <code>production_status.json</code> checksums, loads only plan-compatible analysis,\nand writes one declared schema v2 <code>analysis/instagram_storylines.json</code> artifact\nfor <code>storyline_selection</code> jobs only. It proves the grade-to-story handoff\nbefore real VLM/editorial story intelligence is trusted.\n<code>package-production-local</code> is the deterministic packaging-stage probe: it\nverifies the trusted storyline artifact plus the graded outputs declared for\neach package job, then writes only the declared reel/carousel/photo-set package\noutput. Directory-style package outputs receive a deterministic\n<code>package_manifest.json</code> and directory checksum so retries can reason about the\npackage without treating directories as cleanup authority. All adapters\npersist claims through the single-job production-status compare/write helper, then persist success\nchecksums or retryable errors through the same status boundary. Symlinked\ninputs or outputs, missing inputs, malformed package directories, and\ndifferent existing files become retryable review states instead of cleanup\ninstructions. When existing package directories contain unexpected entries,\nthose relative entry names are included in retryable adapter/scheduler\nmessages so native shells can show the operator what needs review without\ndeleting or trusting the stray bytes.\nThey do not open arbitrary paths, delete files, clean up user outputs, or\ngrant workers authority beyond the trusted production job. The batch runner\nexecutes only trusted action plans, preserves serial production jobs, bounds\nparallel local work, and keeps blocked jobs visible rather than mutating them.\nThe adapter registry also declares output-kind scope for each worker: dry-run\nand metadata adapters can currently accept all planned kinds, while\n<code>proxy-production-local</code> is explicitly limited to <code>proxy_media</code>,\n<code>analysis-production-local</code> to <code>vlm_asset_analysis</code>,\n<code>scene-production-local</code> to <code>scene_grouping</code>, <code>look-production-local</code> to\n<code>look_development</code>, and <code>grade-production-local</code> to\n<code>graded_photo</code>/<code>graded_video</code>, and <code>story-production-local</code> to\n<code>storyline_selection</code>, and <code>package-production-local</code> to <code>instagram_reel</code>,\n<code>instagram_carousel</code>, and <code>instagram_photo_set</code>. Shared result, failure, batch, and error types are named\n<code>ProductionAdapter*</code> rather than <code>DryRunProduction*</code> so future VLM/color/story\nadapters can reuse the same contract without carrying dry-run-specific type\nnames through scheduler code.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_scheduler</code>: the first iterative pre-render scheduler. It\nreloads the trusted production status manifest each pass, optionally expires\nstale running leases through guarded compare/write updates, plans\ndependency-ready actions, runs the registered local production batch adapter,\nand stops with explicit complete/blocked/retryable/failed/waiting state. A job that is\nalready retryable is skipped until the caller explicitly allow-lists that\nreviewed job ID, and a job that becomes retryable during a run is not hammered\nagain in the same run. Stage-specific adapters now narrow planning and\nstale-lease expiration via the capability registry in\n<code>shootflow.production_adapter</code>; stage-specific adapters are offered only jobs\nmatching their declared capabilities and leave downstream story/package stages\npending or running for later adapters rather than converting incompatibility\ninto retryable or blocked state. This protects manual outputs, active workers,\nand future review loops from repeated overwrite attempts.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_handoff</code>: the read-only bridge between plan review and\nactual proxy/VLM/color/story workers. It can still derive a next wave from\ncaller-reported completed IDs for simple demos, but the product path now reads\ntrusted <code>production_status.json</code> via\n<code>plan_next_production_wave_from_status(...)</code>, treating succeeded jobs as\ncompleted, pending jobs as schedulable when dependencies are satisfied, and\nrunning/retryable/failed jobs as non-schedulable state that remains visible\nfor UI or agent review. CLI production-plan and next-wave summaries are schema\nv2 and include root observe-only <code>workflow_authority</code> so a native shell can\nshow candidate production work without granting claim, retry, materialization,\ncleanup, overwrite, deletion, or arbitrary file-mutation authority.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.card_workflow</code>: a product-facing orchestration layer for the first\nMac/native-shell demo. It composes mounted-volume discovery, source-size\nestimation, capacity-gated route recommendation, checksum ingest preview,\noptional confirmed copy, and read-only production reporting while preserving\nthe same no-delete/no-overwrite boundaries as the lower-level commands.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.workspace_index</code>: a metadata-only continuity layer for Mac apps,\nlaunch agents, and future autonomous runs. It inspects a shoot workspace,\nvalidates ingest, production-status, production-scheduler-report, render,\nstatus, and report artifacts through their existing trust boundaries, marks\nstandalone storyline JSON as untrusted until rebound to a production plan, and\ncan persist <code>shootflow_workspace_index.json</code> without creating or deleting any\nmedia pipeline artifacts. Schema v2 includes a root <code>workflow_authority</code>\nobject and carries the derived workspace-health authority in the\n<code>workspace_health</code> artifact; legacy schema v1 indexes load as observe-only.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.story</code>: VLM-score-driven selection planning for first-cut reels, named reel variants,\ncarousels, and ranked photo-set exports, plus a deterministic storyline artifact contract for\ndownstream assemblers.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.assembly</code>: a renderer-neutral handoff from trusted storyline selections to explicit social\nrender tasks. It verifies selected graded artifacts exist, rejects symlinked selected media, keeps raw\noriginals out of render inputs, derives deterministic output paths under <code>deliverables/</code>, chunks large\nranked photo-set exports into parallel resumable tasks, creates collision-free tasks for named reel\nvariants, and persists strict render manifests for future workers.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_status</code>: a render-worker progress contract that persists status keyed by trusted\nrender task IDs and a canonical render-plan fingerprint. It records\npending/running/succeeded/failed/retryable states, renderer adapter names, attempts, error messages,\noutput checksums, and timestamps without accepting source-media paths, arbitrary task IDs, or deletion\ninstructions. Its compare/write update helper takes a local advisory lock, reloads the latest manifest,\nchecks the expected prior state, attempt, and optional <code>updated_at</code> lease token for one task, replaces only\nthat status, and writes back through the same safe manifest writer. Its initialization helper uses the\nsame fingerprint-bound validation and local lock to create the first all-pending status manifest from\ntrusted render tasks only when the status file is missing; matching existing progress is preserved, and\nstale or malformed status files are rejected instead of reset.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_worker</code>: a dry-run worker action planner that consumes trusted <code>AssemblyPlan</code> and\n<code>RenderStatusManifest</code> records, validates the status fingerprint, schedules only dependency-ready\npending/retryable tasks, reports blocked tasks, claims ready tasks as running, and lets adapters report\nsuccess/retry/failure without widening file access. It keeps running tasks out of duplicate scheduling\nuntil a caller explicitly plans stale lease expiration; that lease plan only returns retryable status\ntransitions and never authorizes file cleanup or deletion. Its persistence helper applies those expired\nleases through the same single-task compare/write status boundary, using the original <code>running_since</code>\ntimestamp as the lease token so same-attempt worker heartbeats surface as conflicts instead of being\noverwritten.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_adapter</code>: the first adapter-orchestration layer. Its deterministic dry-run adapter\nconsumes a single trusted <code>RenderWorkerAction</code>, persists a claim through <code>compare_and_write_render_task_status(...)</code>,\nmaterializes only the action&#39;s declared <code>deliverables/</code> outputs as non-overwriting placeholder files, reports\nsuccess checksums or retryable errors, and persists the final status through the same compare/write boundary.\nThe public dispatch path runs only registered adapters (<code>dry-run-local</code> today), so a future ffmpeg/Core\nImage/Metal/DaVinci/cloud backend must be added deliberately behind the same action-plan interface before\nthe scheduler can claim work for it. It also includes a dry-run batch scheduler for a trusted\n<code>RenderWorkerActionPlan</code>: serial-only actions run alone, parallel-safe actions run in ordered groups up to a\ncaller-provided concurrency limit, blocked tasks remain visible and untouched, action fields are rebound to\nthe trusted <code>AssemblyPlan</code> before any output is materialized, and stale scheduler-level action failures are\nreported without materializing outputs. It intentionally does not open source or graded inputs, delete files,\nor implement real media rendering.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_scheduler</code>: a local dry-run orchestration loop above the adapter boundary. It reloads the\ntrusted status manifest on every pass, optionally plans and persists stale-running lease expirations through the\nguarded lease helper, plans dependency-ready actions, runs bounded dry-run batches, and stops with explicit\n<code>complete</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>retryable</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>waiting</code>, or <code>max_iterations</code> reasons. It deliberately skips any task it\nalready attempted when that task becomes retryable in the same run, so a stuck output or manual editor export is\npreserved for review instead of being hammered repeatedly. It can persist a strict\n<code>deliverables/render_scheduler_report.json</code> summary bound to the trusted render-plan fingerprint and final status\nmanifest, giving Mac UI, retry flows, and continuation agents an actionable view of completed, retryable, failed,\nblocked, review-required, materialized-output, lease-conflict, failure-message details, validated product metrics,\nand root observe-only <code>workflow_authority</code> without granting new renderer, retry, cleanup, overwrite, or deletion\nauthority.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.workspace_health</code>: a read-only continuation diagnostic for native\nshells, launch agents, and future agents. It validates production status plus\nproduction scheduler reports from the trusted ingest manifest, then validates\nthe persisted render-task manifest, matching render-status manifest, and render\nscheduler report when enough trusted context exists. It summarizes production\nand render status counts, exposes validated scheduler product metrics when\ntrusted reports exist, and emits a single next safe action. Missing files are\ntreated as workflow state, while invalid files are surfaced for review instead\nof being reset, repaired, or overwritten. Metrics are observational UI/analytics\nstate only; scheduling and file authority stay bound to the underlying trusted\nmanifests and reports. Schema v4 includes the shared observe-only\n<code>workflow_authority</code> object so consumers do not have to infer no-authority\nsemantics from <code>writes_files</code> flags or safety-note prose.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.pilot_dashboard</code>: the first read-only product analytics contract\nabove workspace health. It consumes validated health/report metrics to expose\nactivation flags, metric-source availability, aggregate throughput, manual\nintervention counts, schema v2 pilot acceptance thresholds, and the current\nnext safe action for Mac shells, pilot dashboards, and GTM demos. Missing\nscheduler reports keep metrics unavailable rather than defaulting them to zero.\nAcceptance uses integer basis points over the same trusted metrics and remains\nobservational; it cannot schedule, retry, materialize, repair, overwrite,\nclean up, or delete files. It names the next safe action but omits executable\ncommands for write-capable actions. Serialized dashboard JSON includes a\nstructured <code>workflow_authority</code> object with <code>mode=observe_only</code>,\n<code>handoff_kind=presentation_only</code>, and false booleans for copy, retry,\ncleanup, overwrite, materialization, deletion, and any file mutation so\nnative shells and agents do not have to infer authority from prose.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.pilot_cohort_report</code>: a read-only aggregation contract above saved\nschema v2 pilot-dashboard JSON payloads. It never opens shoot workspaces or\nraw worker manifests; instead it validates each dashboard payload and derives\ncohort-level pilot-ready workspace rate, median intervention rate, first-draft\noutput count, structured readiness-blocker counts, and per-workspace readiness\nevidence. For metrics-available dashboards, it validates threshold booleans\nagainst the reported rates/output count before trusting <code>ready_for_pilot_review</code>,\nso a saved dashboard cannot claim pilot readiness while one component threshold\nis failing. Missing dashboard metrics remain unavailable rather than becoming\nfalse successes or failures, and the cohort report emits no workflow commands.\nIt emits the same\n<code>workflow_authority</code> observe-only object as the dashboard rather than trusting\nany authority claim inside saved dashboard inputs.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.pilot_cohort_brief</code>: a read-only presentation contract above a\nsaved schema v1 pilot cohort report. It validates and reads one saved report,\nthen emits status, headline, summary percentage labels, structured readiness\nblocker counts, controlled blocker labels, and per-workspace cards for native\nshells and GTM demos. Its CLI defaults to JSON for agents and can render a\ndeterministic command-free text view for native-shell demos, omitting saved\npaths, timestamps, workspace titles, and free-form report notes from that text\nsurface while still showing controlled blocker labels. It does not read raw\nworker internals, write files, or emit workflow commands. Its JSON\nrepresentation includes the shared <code>workflow_authority</code> object; the text\nrepresentation keeps authority as plain safety copy and continues omitting\nfree-form saved report fields.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.workflow_authority</code>: the shared no-authority contract reader for\nUI/agent JSON. It emits and validates the root observe-only\n<code>workflow_authority</code> object, and <code>observe_only_handoff_controls_to_dict(...)</code>\nturns any valid observe-only handoff into an all-disabled native control state.\nNative shells should use that control state instead of deriving copy, retry,\ncleanup, overwrite, materialization, deletion, or file-mutation affordances\nfrom <code>next_actions</code>, <code>next_steps</code>, <code>writes_files</code>, <code>agent_handoff</code>, or prose.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.demo_workspace.create_pilot_cohort_cli_demo_workspace</code>: a controlled\nno-real-media fixture for pilot/GTM demos. It creates trusted persisted render\ndemo workspaces, runs the same render init/scheduler path those demos already\nuse, saves schema v2 dashboard JSON files, and writes one saved cohort report.\nIt preflights saved dashboard/report paths before creating shoot workspaces and\nrefuses existing different report files instead of overwriting them.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_workspace</code>: a read-only state index over persisted render\nhandoffs. It starts from strict <code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code>, treats a\nmissing status manifest as <code>needs_status_initialization</code> without creating one,\nvalidates any existing <code>deliverables/render_status.json</code> and\n<code>deliverables/render_scheduler_report.json</code>, rejects stale reports instead of\nusing them as UI truth, treats succeeded tasks with missing deliverable files\nas review-required, and returns task counts, expected/materialized outputs,\nreview-required task IDs, and safe next-action recommendations.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.cli</code>: the first background-agent command surface. The init command loads the persisted\n<code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code> handoff through the same strict task schema/path validation and creates the\nmatching <code>deliverables/render_status.json</code> only when missing, returning a compact JSON summary for a Mac UI or\nlaunch agent. It never deletes media and never resets existing matching status progress. The dry-run scheduler\ncommand then treats the persisted render-task/status pair as the trusted renderer contract, validates the matching\nrender-status manifest by fingerprint, runs the scheduler, writes <code>deliverables/render_scheduler_report.json</code>, and\noptionally prints the same schema-versioned report JSON for a Mac UI, launch agent, or continuation automation.\nNeither command accepts model-authored paths, deletes files, or widens renderer authority beyond the persisted\nrender-task/status contracts.\nThe <code>write-render-tasks</code> command closes the real shoot handoff into that\npersisted renderer contract: it reloads <code>ingest_manifest.json</code>, rebuilds the\nproduction plan, asks <code>shootflow.assembly</code> to verify trusted storyline and\ngraded artifact inputs, then creates or reuses <code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code>\nwithout replacing a different existing manifest or creating deliverable\noutputs. Its JSON summary includes advisory <code>next_actions</code> for status\ninitialization and scheduler execution, including trusted completed dependency\nIDs while keeping runtime timestamps and renderer choices explicit instead of\nbaking them into the manifest. The summary is schema v2 and includes the root\nobserve-only <code>workflow_authority</code> object; write-capable next actions are\ncommand-free descriptors, not permission to initialize, schedule, retry,\nmaterialize, overwrite, clean up, or delete files.\nThe inspection command reads those same handoffs and emits the current product state without initializing,\nscheduling, retrying, deleting, or materializing outputs. Its schema v2\nsummary carries the same root <code>workflow_authority</code> contract.\nThe <code>card-dump-workflow</code> command now exposes the first complete product\nhandoff for local media operations: preview route + ingest plan by default,\nand copy + production summary only when the caller supplies <code>--confirm-ingest</code>.\nConfirmed reports expose advisory <code>next_actions</code> for trusted storyline/graded\npreparation and render-task writing so native shells can guide the user without\nexpanding file authority. The JSON also includes an <code>agent_handoff</code> block so\nlaunch agents and native shells can branch on route repair, preview review, or\nproduction-plan review without interpreting prose notes. Schema v2 root\n<code>workflow_authority</code> remains observe-only, so <code>agent_handoff</code> may describe a\nreviewed next step but cannot authorize copy, retry, cleanup, overwrite,\nmaterialization, deletion, or arbitrary file mutation.\n<code>plan-import-route</code>, <code>preview-ingest</code>, and the confirmed <code>execute-ingest</code>\nreceipt also use schema v2 observe-only payloads; the receipt reports an\nalready-confirmed copy result and grants no further copy or deletion authority.\nThe read-only <code>handoff-controls</code> command wraps the shared authority reader for\nsaved handoff JSON files, returning disabled native file-operation controls\nonly after validating the root observe-only authority object. This gives\nSwiftUI/menu-bar shells a CLI bridge for fail-closed control binding without\nparsing <code>next_actions</code>, <code>next_steps</code>, <code>agent_handoff</code>, or prose.\nThe read-only <code>package-handoff-brief</code> command applies that same control\nbinding to package production reports: it reloads the trusted production plan\nfrom <code>ingest_manifest.json</code>, validates matching production status/report\nfiles, summarizes completed/review-required package jobs and materialized\npackage outputs, and returns disabled copy/retry/materialization controls from\nthe scheduler report authority.\nThe <code>workspace-health</code> command exposes the read-only continuation diagnostic as\nschema-versioned JSON. It is intentionally non-advancing: it may recommend\n<code>init-production-status</code>, <code>dry-run-production-scheduler</code>, production review\nstates, <code>write-render-tasks</code>, <code>init-render-status</code>, or\n<code>dry-run-render-scheduler</code>, but it never performs those writes itself. When a\ntrusted render-task manifest is present, the health summary also fingerprints\nthe current storyline/graded render inputs as metadata only, so UI shells and\nfuture agents can notice in-place input drift without treating the fingerprint\nas new file authority.\nThe <code>index-workspace</code> command adds a single metadata handoff over the current\nworkspace state, including the health-derived next safe action, so native\nshells do not need to infer readiness by probing many files directly or calling\na second command just to decide which reviewed handoff comes next.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.demo_workspace</code>: a demo/native-shell smoke-test helper that creates\na tiny persisted render workspace by writing deterministic placeholder inputs\nand <code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code>. It refuses symlinked roots and existing\ndifferent files, and intentionally leaves status initialization plus scheduler\nreport creation to the normal CLI path so demos exercise the same no-reset\ncontract as the product. Its card-dump demo variant first builds synthetic\ncard/SSD volumes, runs the real confirmed checksum ingest workflow, then seeds\nthe ingested shoot folder with the same render-task demo handoff.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Future native applications should wrap these contracts rather than reimplementing the safety rules in UI code.</p>\n<h2>AI/color pipeline direction</h2>\n<p>The desired high-quality pipeline is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ingest originals to a dated shoot folder.</li>\n<li>Produce a read-only production-plan report from the verified ingest manifest so the operator can see\nasset counts, parallel wave shape, and planned IG deliverables before automation creates new artifacts.</li>\n<li>Generate proxies and technical metadata in parallel.</li>\n<li>Run content understanding: scene grouping, face/object/context tags, sharpness, exposure, motion, audio energy, and duplicate detection.</li>\n<li>Build look references from user presets, past shoots, brand mood boards, or a hero frame.</li>\n<li>Generate non-destructive color decisions per scene/set, not isolated per asset.</li>\n<li>Rank storylines from VLM outputs so first-cut reels and carousels have narrative and scene variety.</li>\n<li>Export graded intermediates for all selects.</li>\n<li>Plan and persist assembly tasks from trusted graded selections before invoking any renderer.</li>\n<li>Initialize and track render-worker progress through a resumable status manifest keyed by the trusted task plan.</li>\n<li>Plan renderer-neutral worker actions from the trusted task/status pair.</li>\n<li>Claim ready render actions as running, then report success/retry/failure statuses with checksums for\ndeclared deliverable outputs only.</li>\n<li>Persist each worker transition through a single-task compare/write helper so stale workers cannot\noverwrite newer claims or results.</li>\n<li>Plan stale running leases from trusted status plus an explicit cutoff, then persist each retryable\ntransition through the same single-task compare/write boundary.</li>\n<li>Run adapter orchestration against a registered dry-run renderer that proves claim/result persistence and\noutput-safety rules before wiring in real ffmpeg, Core Image/Metal, DaVinci Resolve, or cloud GPU rendering.</li>\n<li>Run trusted ready-action batches with bounded local concurrency so high-volume photo-set chunks can make\nprogress while serial reels and blocked tasks stay deterministic and inspectable.</li>\n<li>Drive the dry-run renderer with an iterative scheduler loop that alternates trusted status reloads, stale lease\nexpiration, ready-action planning, bounded batch execution, and explicit stop summaries for UI/retry decisions.</li>\n<li>Persist a scheduler report after each run so UI/retry/continuation layers can tell the user which deliverables\nfinished, which tasks need review, which outputs were materialized, and which failures or lease conflicts require\nattention.</li>\n<li>Run <code>write-render-tasks</code>, persisted-manifest init, and scheduler commands\nthrough a CLI/background-agent flow so native app work can begin from durable\nhandoffs rather than an in-process Python object graph.</li>\n<li>Inspect the persisted render workspace before and after scheduler runs so\nnative app shells and background agents can show a safe next action without\nmutating status or trusting stale scheduler reports.</li>\n<li>Drive the route -&gt; preview -&gt; optional ingest -&gt; production-summary path\nthrough a single <code>card-dump-workflow</code> command so native UI shells can prove\nthe card-dump experience without reimplementing storage or ingest safety.\nKeep the next orchestration step machine-readable (<code>agent_handoff</code> block)\nso background agents can remain conservative around human review and copy\nauthority.</li>\n<li>Keep a small persisted-manifest demo workspace helper available so Mac UI\nshells, launch-agent smoke tests, and GTM demos can exercise the init-to-report\nflow without constructing trusted render tasks by hand; use the card-dump\ndemo variant when the demo must also cover route discovery and checksum ingest.</li>\n<li>Initialize production-status manifests from verified ingest manifests so\npre-render workers can resume production waves without confusing\ncaller-reported completed IDs with durable job progress.</li>\n<li>Use production-worker action/status helpers to claim proxy/VLM/color/story\njobs from trusted status, checksum declared outputs before success, and\nreport retry/failure without giving workers file cleanup or deletion\nauthority.</li>\n<li>Exercise those helpers through the registered local production adapters:\nthe dry-run adapter materializes only declared placeholder file artifacts,\nwhile <code>metadata-production-local</code> writes deterministic metadata JSON from\nworkspace-bound trusted inputs. <code>proxy-production-local</code> byte-copies trusted\nlocal source inputs into declared proxy outputs as a safety-proven stand-in\nbefore a real proxy renderer is trusted. <code>analysis-production-local</code> writes\ndeterministic, schema-compatible per-asset analysis JSON for\n<code>vlm_asset_analysis</code> jobs only, proving the analysis output/status handoff\nbefore real local or hosted VLM runners are trusted. <code>scene-production-local</code>\nmaterializes the first deterministic serial rejoin artifact,\n<code>analysis/scene_groups.json</code>, from checksum-verified trusted analysis\noutputs. <code>look-production-local</code> materializes the next deterministic serial\nhandoff, <code>color/shoot_look.json</code>, from the checksum-verified scene summary.\n<code>grade-production-local</code> materializes deterministic per-asset graded\nstand-ins from the trusted original, schema-validated/checksum-verified\nanalysis, and schema-validated/checksum-verified look artifact.\n<code>story-production-local</code> materializes the deterministic storyline-selection\nartifact from checksum-verified analysis, scene, look, and graded inputs\nwhile leaving package jobs for compatible future adapters.\nAll adapters persist success or retryable state through\n<code>production_status.json</code> before real VLM, color, or storyline runners are\ntrusted.</li>\n<li>Run the dry-run production scheduler from persisted status so Mac shells and\nbackground agents can advance proxy, analysis, look, grading, storyline, and\npackage jobs without reconstructing the worker loop in UI code. Retryable,\nfailed, or blocked jobs remain review states, not cleanup authority; prior\nretryable jobs require an explicit reviewed retry allow-list. Stale running\njobs can be expired only through explicit cutoff inputs and guarded\npreconditions, preserving heartbeat-refresh conflicts for review.</li>\n<li>Build a workspace health summary or workspace index after ingest,\nproduction scheduling, render scheduling, or retries so UI and continuation\nagents can show one coherent shoot-state snapshot while keeping every\nauthoritative decision bound to the underlying trusted manifests.</li>\n<li>Compare metadata-only render input fingerprints across health/index snapshots\nso automation can flag changed storyline or graded bytes before relying on\nolder render status/report context.</li>\n<li>Assemble deliverables: reels, carousels, photo sets, story crops, thumbnails, captions, and suggested posting packages.</li>\n<li>Iterate using reviewer feedback: &quot;warmer skin,&quot; &quot;more cinematic contrast,&quot; &quot;use more behind-the-scenes,&quot; &quot;make it faster.&quot;</li>\n</ol>\n<p>High quality depends on scene-level consistency and reversible edits. The system should produce edit decision data first, then render from masters.</p>\n<h2>Parallelism model</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Per-asset analysis and initial grading jobs are embarrassingly parallel.</li>\n<li>Scene clustering, look normalization, and deliverable assembly depend on per-asset outputs.</li>\n<li>Reels should assemble from proxy timelines, then conform from graded masters.</li>\n<li>The job graph should be deterministic and resumable so 10,000+ asset shoots can recover from interruption without starting over.</li>\n<li>The production planner uses explicit execution waves:<ol>\n<li>proxy every asset;</li>\n<li>run per-asset VLM/content analysis;</li>\n<li>group scenes and narrative sets;</li>\n<li>derive a shoot-level look;</li>\n<li>render per-asset graded masters in parallel;</li>\n<li>rank IG storylines from analysis, scene, and look data;</li>\n<li>assemble social packages from the graded masters.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>This structure keeps expensive per-asset work highly parallel while preserving quality-critical global\ndecisions, especially scene consistency and look development.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Production planning notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Job IDs are deterministic for a fixed manifest order so later workers can cache, retry, and resume\nindividual assets within a planned shoot. A future manifest-backed ID should incorporate relative path\nand checksum when the ingest manifest becomes the canonical production input.</li>\n<li>Ingest manifests are the handoff contract from file operations to AI/editing work. Catalog loading\nvalidates that manifest destinations stay under the import root, rejects destructive-source manifests,\nrechecks destination size/checksum, and ignores non-photo/video assets for production planning while\npreserving them in the original ingest manifest.</li>\n<li>VLM analysis files are not trusted just because they exist. <code>shootflow.analysis</code> loads only the\n<code>analysis/00000.json</code> style files declared by the production plan, requires each file to name the\nplanned source asset, rejects unplanned numbered analysis files, validates scores/durations/tags, and\nemits <code>AssetAnalysis</code> records that point at graded artifacts rather than originals.</li>\n<li>Jobs expose explicit output artifacts. Instagram deliverables consume graded-master artifacts, not raw\noriginals, which keeps social assembly downstream of the color pipeline.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_handoff</code> is the read-only bridge between plan review and\nactual proxy/VLM/color/story workers. It reloads the verified ingest manifest,\nrebuilds the deterministic production plan, canonicalizes completed job IDs\ninto plan order, rejects unknown IDs, duplicates, and completed jobs whose\ndependencies are not also complete, then returns the next ready wave. The\nlegacy path accepts caller-reported completed IDs for simple demos; the product\npath loads the matching trusted <code>production_status.json</code> and derives completed\nIDs from succeeded statuses while keeping retryable jobs in the review-required\nset instead of handing them back as automatic work. It deliberately writes no\nanalysis, graded, render-task, status, scheduler-report, or deliverable files.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_worker</code> is the mutation boundary after the read-only\nproduction handoff. It accepts only a trusted production plan plus matching\nstatus manifest, schedules pending/retryable jobs whose dependencies have\nsucceeded, and returns blocked-job reasons for UI/agent visibility. Workers can\nclaim a matching pending/retryable status as running, report success only after\nevery declared output file exists and checksums, or report retry/failure with a\nclear error message. Persisted status transitions should use\n<code>compare_and_write_production_job_status(...)</code>, which checks expected state,\nattempt, and optional <code>updated_at</code> lease token while holding the local status\nlock. Stale lease persistence uses that same precondition so an interrupted\nworker can be marked retryable without overwriting a same-attempt heartbeat\nrefresh from an active worker.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_adapter</code> now proves that persistence path without real\nmedia or model execution. The dry-run adapter rebinds each action to the\ntrusted production job before any status mutation, persists a running claim\nwith the observed prior state/attempt/timestamp, writes deterministic\nplaceholder bytes only to declared file output paths, refuses symlinked,\ndirectory-style, or existing different outputs, and records retryable errors\nthat explicitly preserve the no-delete/no-cleanup boundary. Adapter capability\nmetadata lives here rather than in the scheduler, so future VLM, color,\nstoryline, and packaging workers can declare supported <code>output_kind</code> values\nbehind the adapter contract before the scheduler offers them work.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_scheduler</code> composes that adapter into a bounded local\nloop. It does not invent job progress from command-line IDs; each iteration\nreloads <code>production_status.json</code>, plans ready pending/retryable work from the\ntrusted <code>ProductionPlan</code>, dispatches a dry-run batch with a caller-supplied\nconcurrency cap, and stops when the status is complete, blocked, failed,\nwaiting, or retryable work needs review. Previously retryable jobs are skipped\nunless their IDs are explicitly supplied as reviewed retry candidates. When a\nlease cutoff is supplied, stale running jobs are first expired into retryable\nreview state and heartbeat-refresh conflicts are preserved. The\npersisted <code>production_scheduler_report.json</code> is validated against the trusted\nproduction plan and final <code>production_status.json</code>, then records attempted,\nskipped retryable, expired-lease, conflicted-lease, completed, retryable,\nfailed, blocked, review-required, materialized-output, and failure-message\ndetails for UI/continuation review. It also emits validated product metric\ncounts derived from those same fields, so metrics cannot be forged into product\ntruth without matching the final trusted status. Its root observe-only\n<code>workflow_authority</code> makes the saved post-operation report presentation-only;\nretry, cleanup, materialization, overwrite, and deletion still require a\nseparate reviewed action path.</li>\n<li>Per-asset proxy and graded artifacts are namespaced by job ID so common camera filenames such as\n<code>IMG_0001.CR3</code> do not collide across cards or archive folders.</li>\n<li>Deliverables depend on matching graded masters, not raw originals. Reels only depend on video grades;\ncarousels and photo sets only depend on photo grades.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.story</code> currently chooses a first-cut reel from the best clip per scene, then adds named\n<code>hero</code>, <code>behind_the_scenes</code>, and <code>fast_cut</code> reel variants for reviewable social narratives. Carousels\ncome from diversified photo selects, and photo sets come from every graded photo ranked by quality.\nThis keeps the automatic output useful while still preserving all graded intermediates for human/editor\nreview.</li>\n<li>Storyline selection persists to <code>analysis/instagram_storylines.json</code> as an edit-decision contract\nwith reel, reel-variant, carousel, and photo-set sequences. The reader rejects references outside the\ntrusted graded artifact set so model-authored JSON cannot smuggle arbitrary files into a renderer.</li>\n<li>Assembly planning consumes that storyline artifact and rechecks selected graded files on disk before\nproducing render tasks. Reel tasks use selected graded videos only; carousel and photo-set tasks use\nselected graded photos only. Outputs are deterministic (<code>deliverables/instagram_reel.mp4</code>,\nnamed reel variants under <code>deliverables/instagram_reels/</code>, ordered carousel slides, and ordered\nphoto-set JPEGs) so retries and UI review do not reshuffle the package.</li>\n<li><code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code> is the render-worker handoff. The loader requires exact JSON fields,\nsafe analysis/graded inputs, deliverables-only outputs, no symlinked manifest/temp paths, no duplicate\noutput targets, and an exact match with the trusted in-memory <code>AssemblyPlan</code> before a renderer may\nconsume it.</li>\n<li>Large <code>photo_set</code> sequences are split into deterministic chunk tasks such as\n<code>export_instagram_photo_set_part_00001</code> while preserving public output names like\n<code>deliverables/instagram_photo_set/photo_00001.jpg</code>. This lets 10,000-photo exports retry and parallelize\nat chunk granularity without changing the user-visible package structure.</li>\n<li>The current planner is intentionally an edit-decision contract, not a renderer. Future renderers can be\nlocal Metal/Core Image, ffmpeg/LUT, DaVinci Resolve automation, or cloud GPU workers as long as they\nhonor the same job IDs and dependencies.</li>\n<li>Renderer adapters should accept only trusted <code>RenderTask</code> records, verify each input still exists before\nopening it, write outputs through temporary files before atomic replacement, and report per-task status\nwithout deleting source or graded media. A Core Image/Metal adapter, ffmpeg adapter, DaVinci Resolve\nautomation adapter, or cloud GPU adapter should all be swappable behind that contract.</li>\n<li><code>deliverables/render_status.json</code> is the companion worker-progress handoff. The loader requires exact\nJSON fields, the same shoot slug, task ordering, and render-plan fingerprint as the trusted\n<code>AssemblyPlan</code>, known status states, state-appropriate attempts/error/checksum fields, symlink-safe\nmanifest paths, and checksums only for each task&#39;s expected deliverable outputs. Status writes use\nexclusive no-follow temporary file creation and reject stale canonical temp files. Status validation\nreuses full assembly-plan validation before fingerprinting so in-memory callers cannot bypass the\nrender-task manifest&#39;s path/dependency rules. Status files cannot introduce source paths, new tasks,\nstale task contents, or destructive operations. Workers should persist status transitions through\n<code>compare_and_write_render_task_status(...)</code>, which holds a local advisory lock across reload,\nprecondition check, replacement, and write. The helper verifies the task&#39;s current state and attempt still\nmatch the transition precondition, can also require the current <code>updated_at</code> value to match an\n<code>expected_updated_at</code> lease token, replaces exactly one status, preserves task order/fingerprint/slug, and\nwrites through the safe manifest writer. This protects same-workspace local workers from last-writer-wins\nstatus clobbering and lets stale lease expiration fail if a same-attempt heartbeat refreshed the task after\nplanning. The initialization helper uses that same local lock to safely bootstrap a missing status manifest\nfrom trusted render tasks, while loading matching existing status as-is and rejecting stale fingerprints\ninstead of resetting progress; distributed/cloud workers still need an external lease or storage-native\ncompare-and-swap around the same contract.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_worker</code> is the next handoff after status validation, not a renderer. It accepts a\ncaller-provided set of completed non-render dependency IDs from the trusted production/job executor,\ntreats render-task dependencies as satisfied only by succeeded render statuses, plans attempts for\npending/retryable tasks, and returns blocked-task reasons for UI or scheduler visibility. It also exposes\nexplicit status-transition helpers: a worker can claim a pending/retryable action only when the current\nstatus and attempt number match, report success only after every declared deliverable output exists and\nhas a SHA-256 checksum, or report retry/failure with a clear error message. It intentionally leaves real\nmedia opening, output temp-file strategy, output cleanup, and cross-process file locking to future\nrenderer adapters. <code>plan_stale_render_leases(...)</code> is the conservative timeout boundary: callers provide a\ntimezone-aware cutoff and the helper returns retryable transitions for running statuses whose <code>updated_at</code>\nis older than that cutoff. It skips fresh running work, validates the trusted status manifest first, and\nincludes an explicit no-cleanup/no-deletion error message so a timeout cannot be confused with authority\nto remove source, graded, or deliverable files. Persist each expiration with <code>expected_state=&quot;running&quot;</code>,\nthe lease&#39;s attempt number, and <code>expected_updated_at=running_since</code> so a refreshed same-attempt lease cannot\nbe expired by a stale scheduler snapshot. <code>persist_stale_render_leases(...)</code> now packages that persistence\npattern for schedulers: successfully expired tasks become retryable, while compare/write precondition\nfailures are returned as task-level conflicts that explicitly say another worker already changed the task\nand no source, graded, or deliverable files were deleted.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_adapter</code> now exercises the adapter handoff without real media rendering. The dry-run\nadapter loads the latest trusted status manifest, claims a matching pending/retryable action, writes\ndeterministic placeholder outputs only for declared deliverable paths, refuses symlinked outputs, refuses\nto overwrite any existing file with different bytes, and then reports success checksums or retryable\nmaterialization errors. It uses hard-link commit from a same-directory temp file instead of blind replace\nso a user/editor export that appears at the target path is preserved. This is the safety shape future\nhigh-quality renderers should keep while replacing placeholder bytes with real media output. Its batch\nhelper executes only a prevalidated <code>RenderWorkerActionPlan</code>, respects each action&#39;s <code>can_run_in_parallel</code>\nflag, caps parallel groups with <code>max_concurrency</code>, surfaces blocked task IDs, verifies action fields against\nthe trusted render tasks, and records stale-action failures with an explicit no-deletion message instead of\ntreating them as render results.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_scheduler</code> is the first end-to-end local scheduler shape for the future Mac app. It runs the\ntrusted dry-run render loop until no immediately runnable work remains, including stale-running lease recovery\nwhen the caller supplies an explicit cutoff. It reports final succeeded/retryable/failed/blocked task IDs and\na stop reason instead of hiding unfinished work. Retryable tasks attempted during the same scheduler run are not\nretried again in a tight loop, preserving manual exports and unsafe output conflicts for user/editor review.\n<code>write_dry_run_render_scheduler_report(...)</code> turns the in-memory result plus the final trusted status manifest into\n<code>deliverables/render_scheduler_report.json</code>, and the loader rejects stale fingerprints, unknown task IDs, output paths\nnot present in succeeded status checksums, and inconsistent review-required task lists. This report is product-facing\nstate, not a renderer authority: renderers still consume trusted render tasks and status manifests. Schema v2 added\nderived product metrics to the report and validates them against the task/status fields instead of trusting caller\nsupplied analytics. Schema v3 adds root observe-only <code>workflow_authority</code> so native shells and continuation agents can\nfail closed when opening the persisted scheduler report directly; legacy v1/v2 reports are loaded as observe-only.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.render_workspace</code> is the read-only UI/agent state surface over those persisted handoffs. Inspection\nnow verifies succeeded deliverable bytes against status-manifest SHA-256 checksums, not just path presence, and\nroutes missing or checksum-mismatched outputs to <code>review_render_outputs</code> instead of reporting the workspace complete.\nIt still performs no initialization, scheduling, overwrite, cleanup, or deletion work.</li>\n<li><code>shootflow.production_adapter</code> now has real local adapter proofs behind the\nsame production-worker action/status contract as the dry-run adapter.\n<code>metadata-production-local</code> reads trusted local inputs, records file size and\nSHA-256 metadata, and writes deterministic JSON only to declared output files.\n<code>proxy-production-local</code> copies trusted local source bytes into declared proxy\noutputs as a reversible stand-in for future ffmpeg/Metal proxy generation.\nMissing inputs, directory-style outputs, stale claims, symlinks, or output\nconflicts route back to retry/review status without deleting or cleaning up\nmedia.</li>\n<li>VLM output should feed structured scene, subject, quality, and narrative metadata. It should not make\ndestructive storage decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Business viability notes</h2>\n<p>Primary buyers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Photographers and videographers who need more Instagram output but want time back from culling, editing, and file management.</li>\n<li>Small studios already paying editors who want faster turnaround, better consistency, and more content per dollar.</li>\n<li>Creators with large archives who want old footage turned into new reels and carousels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Useful wedges:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Dump a card, get organized imports and draft IG packs.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Turn a wedding/editorial/event shoot into 3 reels, 2 carousels, and graded selects overnight.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Consistent look across photo and video without managing files by hand.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Know exactly what is ready, rendering, blocked, or safe to retry in a 10,000-asset shoot.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Hand an editor or renderer a deterministic task list, progress file, and ready-action queue instead of\na vague drive full of media.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Let local or cloud workers claim and report render tasks without ever receiving authority to delete\noriginals or invent output paths.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Reject stale local worker results at the manifest boundary when another worker has already claimed,\nretried, or completed the same task.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Prove renderer orchestration with a dry-run adapter before customers trust it with irreplaceable shoots or\neditor-produced deliverables.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Safely chew through ready render-action batches on local hardware while keeping blocked, stale, and\ninterrupted work visible instead of hidden inside an opaque export queue.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Recover interrupted local render work by expiring stale leases through the same guarded status update path,\nwhile preserving heartbeat-refresh wins from active workers.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Avoid competing only on generic AI editing. The differentiator is end-to-end workflow: ingest safety, organization, grade consistency, social packaging, and iteration.</p>\n<h2>Open decisions for future iterations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Native stack: Swift/SwiftUI plus a Python/Rust worker, or a Tauri/Electron shell with native media extensions.</li>\n<li>Color engine: LUT/CDL/ACES-style decision layer, Core Image/Metal renderer, DaVinci Resolve integration, or hybrid.</li>\n<li>VLM providers: local model support for privacy vs. hosted APIs for quality and throughput.</li>\n<li>Sync model between Mac and iPhone.</li>\n<li>Pricing: one-time pro tool, subscription, or usage-based render credits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New decisions (current iteration)</h2>\n<h3>Decision: prioritize pro service providers first</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> working pros (wedding/event/photo-video teams) have persistent\nworkflow pain, existing spend, and stronger willingness to pay versus casual\ncreator users.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> feature prioritization should bias toward ingest reliability,\nbatch safety, repeatable quality, and fast review/handoff over broad social\nnovelty features.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: Mac-first product, iPhone as companion</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> SD/SSD ingest, large local catalogs, and heavy parallel media\nworkflows are desktop-native jobs. iPhone remains valuable for review and\napprovals, but not as the core execution runtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> keep the core pipeline local-first and deterministic so a\nfuture SwiftUI desktop shell and background agent can orchestrate without\ncloud lock-in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: destination capacity gate for route recommendation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> static &quot;writable destination&quot; checks are not sufficient for\n10,000-asset shoots. Route recommendation should optionally enforce estimated\nimport bytes plus safety headroom before marking a route review-ready.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>recommend_import_route_with_capacity(...)</code> now exists as the\nhigh-volume recommendation entry point; UI/agent layers should prefer it when\nan estimated import size is available.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: mount-root discovery before native drive APIs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> before wiring Swift, Disk Arbitration, or iPhone import APIs,\nthe product needs a small tested contract that says which filesystem entries\ncan become route candidates. Scanning immediate children of reviewed roots\nkeeps the core portable and avoids granting broad filesystem authority.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>discover_storage_candidates(...)</code> and\n<code>discover_import_route(...)</code> are the Mac-ingest bridge for now. A future native\nadapter can provide richer removable/read-only metadata, but it should still\npreserve the symlink skip, no-delete, review-required, and capacity-gated\nhandoff semantics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: native macOS metadata still feeds the same reviewed route contract</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> Disk Arbitration/Swift can identify ejectable, removable,\ninternal, network, read-only, and system volumes more accurately than a\nportable <code>/Volumes</code> directory scan, but richer metadata should not create a\nseparate authority path for ingest.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>MacOSVolumeMetadata</code> and\n<code>recommend_import_route_from_macos_metadata(...)</code> are pure adapters. They\nfilter unsafe native records and then reuse <code>StorageCandidate</code> ranking plus\ncapacity-gated route recommendation. Future Mac UI work should pass native\nfacts through this adapter, then still require source/destination review and\nexplicit ingest confirmation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: CLI offload planning should support source-size-derived capacity gating</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> high-volume users need a conservative pre-ingest answer to\n&quot;is this destination safe for this card right now?&quot; without manually estimating\nsource payload size.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>shootflow.cli plan-import-route</code> now supports\n<code>--estimate-source-bytes</code>, which discovers a likely source, estimates media\nbytes from that source, and reapplies route recommendation with capacity\nheadroom checks. The command remains advisory-only, review-required, and\nnon-destructive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: ingest CLI requires explicit --confirm before copying</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> background agents, CI runners, and Mac launch agents should\nnever trigger a card copy by accident. A separate preview command provides\nthe JSON the operator reviews before any bytes move.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>shootflow.cli preview-ingest</code> plans an ingest and prints\nplanned items with SHA-256 checksums and sizes but never copies bytes.\n<code>shootflow.cli execute-ingest</code> refuses to run without <code>--confirm</code> and prints\na friendly stderr hint pointing at <code>preview-ingest</code>. With <code>--confirm</code> it\ndefers to the existing <code>plan_import</code> + <code>execute_copy_plan</code> safety contract\n(no source deletion, no overwriting different bytes, SHA-256-verified copies,\ndeterministic <code>ingest_manifest.json</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: ingest CLI emits indented JSON for Mac UI consumption</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> the future Mac UI / SwiftUI menu-bar app and shell scripting\nsurface both want stable, sorted, indented JSON they can pretty-print or\nre-parse without invoking Python.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>preview-ingest</code> and <code>execute-ingest</code> emit\n<code>json.dumps(..., indent=2, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=True)</code> and never log\nfree-form text on the success path. Errors still surface as\n<code>shootflow: &lt;message&gt;</code> on stderr with exit code 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: persisted CLI demos should use the real manifest/status path</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> Mac UI work and GTM proof demos need a repeatable workspace, but\na bespoke demo shortcut would hide the safety contracts that differentiate the\nproduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>shootflow.demo_workspace.create_persisted_cli_demo_workspace</code>\nwrites only placeholder trusted inputs and <code>deliverables/render_tasks.json</code>;\ndemos must still call <code>init-render-status</code> and <code>dry-run-render-scheduler</code> to\ncreate progress and reports through the same strict persisted-manifest path as\nproduction workflows.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision: card-dump reports expose machine-readable agent handoff state</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> accepted</li>\n<li><strong>Rationale:</strong> The future Mac app, menu-bar agent, and cron-style automations\nshould not infer copy authority or review state from human-readable notes.\nThe route -&gt; preview -&gt; confirmed ingest flow is especially sensitive because\na wrong branch can move large media payloads before the operator reviews the\ndestination.</li>\n<li><strong>Consequence:</strong> <code>card-dump-workflow</code> success JSON includes <code>agent_handoff</code>\nwith a stable <code>next_step</code>, <code>human_review_required</code>, <code>safe_to_rerun</code>,\n<code>next_step_may_copy_bytes</code>, and <code>requires_confirm_ingest</code>. The values remain\nconservative: blocked routes require review, preview completion points at\nhuman review before a copy-capable confirmed run, and post-ingest reports\npoint at production-plan review rather than downstream AI writes.</li>\n</ul>\n";

